  "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
  "model": "gpt-6-luna",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_luna_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B008",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "For the development and realization of optical lattice clocks."
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B038",
    "B012"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "Optical lattice clocks made extraordinarily precise optical frequency comparisons possible and established a durable, reproducible measurement method. Katori's lattice-clock concept and Ye's complementary experimental advances provide a focused attribution. The achievement has already opened a new measurement regime and supports fundamental tests; its merit does not depend on future applications or on the latest precision records.",
    "case_against": "The field's development involved many groups, so the committee must distinguish the foundational clock concept and its realization from later improvements. Danielsson's question about whether a mature instrument is enough is important: the case should rest on the completed achievement of making optical lattice clocks work as a precision method, rather than on tests still underway. The breadth of subsequent contributions remains an attribution question.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson questioned whether the clocks' demonstrated measurement capability is a sufficiently bounded prize subject when some of the most consequential fundamental tests remain an active program.",
        "response": "I take that concern seriously. The foundational advance is itself complete: optical lattice clocks made exceptionally precise optical frequency comparisons possible and have been repeatedly realized. The prize need not wait for a particular application, but the citation should identify that clock development rather than claim credit for future tests."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson emphasized separating the foundational clock concept from incremental improvements and asked whether the proposed boundary represents the early work fairly.",
        "response": "I agree. Katori's lattice-clock concept and Ye's complementary experimental advances define the contribution I would recognize; later gains in precision show the method's reach but do not expand the attribution to every subsequent advance in optical metrology."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig argued that the exact nonequilibrium relations are foundational and experimentally tested, while asking that their impact beyond statistical physics be weighed against other achievements.",
        "response": "The relations are an important theoretical achievement, and their exact, testable connection between nonequilibrium work and equilibrium free-energy differences is compelling. I retain B008 because its experimental realization and repeated use establish a distinct measurement capability with broad reach. My assessment is based on those scientific merits, not on how many members favor either proposal."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Historical review should clarify the precise foundational contributions of Katori and Ye and how they are distinguished from later work by other groups."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "I have a long-standing close collaboration with Anne L'Huillier, a 2023 Physics laureate. That connection is unrelated to B008 or its proposed laureates. The supplied profile does not establish whether I served on the committee in 2023; I disclose the connection for completeness."
  }
}
